\
> What software did you use to create your data visualization?\
	I used Excel and the pivot table function\
\
    > Who is your intended audience? \
    	Policy makers, but also the public\
\
    > What information or message are you trying to convey with your visualization? \
    	I wanted to compliment my initial viz by showing that when we look at monthly averages, in Toronto in 2024, patients requiring ICU support are a tiny fraction of all hospitalizations due to COVID. This bit of information, alongside the first visualization, makes a case for allocating more resources to the ICUs where there is a great infrastructure need to keep patients alive.\
\
    > What aspects of design did you consider when making your visualization? How did you apply them? With what elements of your plots? \
    	I wanted to use stacked columns for the monthly averages to show proportionality. \
\
    > How did you ensure that your data visualizations are reproducible? If the tool you used to make your data visualization is not reproducible, how will this impact your data visualization? \
    	I am providing the raw data with the pivot table organization intact\'97this should ensure reproducibility \
\
    > How did you ensure that your data visualization is accessible?  \
    	I used a colourblind friendly palette (blue and orange) \
\
    > Who are the individuals and communities who might be impacted by your visualization?  \
    	This viz makes the point that the hospital system in Toronto had a minor fraction of COVID19 cases requiring ICU care with respect to all COVID19 hospitalizations. Hospital staff can review this information to allocate funds to better equip the ICUs (by investing in more ventilators in case of a new breakout)\
\
    > How did you choose which features of your chosen dataset to include or exclude from your visualization? \
    	There were several other geographic regions in this dataset. Additionally, the datapoints ranged from 2020-2024. I focused on my own geographic area and used the most recent year as my datapoints. I wanted to keep my focus consistent across my two visualizations. \
